[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105935,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelioid haemangioma is a benign tumour of well-formed blood vessels lined by epithelioid endothelial cells with abundant cytoplasm and a variable eosinophilic infiltrate.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105936,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9125/0 Epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9125/0 Cellular epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9125/0 Atypical epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9125/0 Intravascular epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105937,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E81.0Y &amp; XH10T4 Other specified neoplastic haemangioma &amp; Epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105938,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> angiolymphoid hyperplasia with eosinophilia; histiocytoid haemangioma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105939,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular epithelioid haemangioma; atypical epithelioid haemangioma; intravascular epithelioid haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105940,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cutaneous tumours usually involve the head and neck {4008683}. Soft tissue tumours usually involve the extremities {26135557}. Osseous lesions usually involve tubular bones {18852673}. Penile cases are rare {31693501; 15087672; 26135557}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105941,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with cutaneous tumours present with one or sometimes multiple erythematous to violaceous papule(s) or nodule(s) {4008683; 29266025}. In children, the lower extremities are commonly involved and may be multifocal {30207085}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105942,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most cases occur in adults. In children, the mean patient age is 14&nbsp;years (range: 2&ndash;18&nbsp;years) {30207085}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105943,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. A subset may be due to a reactive process secondary to an underlying arteriovenous malformation or traumatic pseudoaneurysm {1924276}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105944,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rearrangements of <em>FOS</em> or <em>FOSB</em> are seen in approximately half of all cases {26135557}. Fusion-positive tumours are usually soft tissue and osseous tumours {26135557; 26173738}. Fusion partners for <em>FOS</em> include <em>LMNA</em>, <em>MBNL1</em>, <em>VIM</em>, and lincRNA. For <em>FOSB</em> the fusion partner is typically <em>ZFP36</em> and rarely <em>WWTR1</em> or <em>ACTB</em> {25043949; 26135557; 26173738}. The FOS family of transcription factors are leucine zipper proteins that dimerize with JUN-family proteins. In <em>FOS</em> fusions, there is loss of the C-terminal part, preventing rapid degradation of FOS and prolonged activation {29150442}. <em>FOSB</em> fusions result in activating promoter-swap, leading to the upregulation of FOSB {28797505}. Cutaneous tumours lack fusions {26135557}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105945,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "<p>Epithelioid haemangioma is usually small (most are &lt;&nbsp;20&nbsp;mm, range: 5&ndash;70&nbsp;mm) {4008683; 18852673}. Cutaneous/soft tissue tumours are usually unremarkable. Osseous tumours are haemorrhagic and may expand the bone {18852673}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-04-28T17:31:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105946,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelioid haemangioma is composed of a lobular proliferation of well-formed blood vessels lined by endothelial cells with enlarged vesicular nuclei, small nucleoli, and abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm. Intracytoplasmic vacuoles may be present. There is no multilayering, and vessels have a pericyte layer. The stroma is variably oedematous and may have a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with eosinophils. A subset have more solid growth and atypia (atypical epithelioid haemangioma) {25043949}. Intravascular epithelioid haemangioma arises within veins {32094426}. They express CD31, CD34, ERG, and frequently FOSB (in ~50% of cases) {28009608}. EMA and keratin immunoreactivity is common {8333560; 21904630; 21317715}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105947,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105948,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Identification of <em>FOS</em> or <em>FOSB</em> rearrangement is useful in a subset {26135557; 26173738}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105949,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> well-formed vessels lined by epithelioid endothelial cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> variable eosinophilic infiltrate; immunohistochemical expression of panendothelial markers and FOSB; <em>FOS</em> or <em>FOSB</em> rearrangement in selected cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105950,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105951,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 462,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelioid haemangiomas are benign, but local recurrence is common.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:43:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]